"Shifted Composition I: Harnack and Reverse Transport Inequalities (https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.14520)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Title: Shifted Composition I: Harnack and Reverse Transport Inequalities ... Altschuler, Sinho Chewi ... Our approach bridges continuous-time coupling methods from geometric analysis with the discrete-time shifted divergence technique from differential privacy and sampling.\nTitle: Shifted Composition I: Harnack and Reverse Transport Inequalities\nAuthors: Jason M. Altschuler, Sinho Chewi\nDate: Fri Nov 24 14:46:48 2023\n\nWe formulate a new information-theoretic principle--the shifted composition rule--which bounds the divergence (e.g., Kullback-Leibler or R\\'enyi) between the laws of two stochastic processes via the introduction of auxiliary shifts. In this paper, we apply this principle to prove reverse transport inequalities for diffusions which, by duality, imply F.-Y. Wang's celebrated dimension-free Harnack inequalities. Our approach bridges continuous-time coupling methods from geometric analysis with the discrete-time shifted divergence technique from differential privacy and sampling. It also naturally gives rise to (1) an alternative continuous-time coupling method based on optimal transport, which bypasses Girsanov transformations, (2) functional inequalities for discrete-time processes, and (3) a family of \"reverse\" Harnack inequalities.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShifted Composition II: Shift Harnack Inequalities and Curvature Upper Bounds (https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.00071)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Altschuler, Sinho Chewi ... We apply the shifted composition rule -- an information-theoretic principle introduced in our earlier work [AC23] -- to establish shift Harnack inequalities for the Langevin diffusion. ... Finally, we show that the local gradient-entropy inequality implies optimal concentration of the score, a.k.a. the logarithmic gradient of the density.\nTitle: Shifted Composition II: Shift Harnack Inequalities and Curvature Upper Bounds\nAuthors: Jason M. Altschuler, Sinho Chewi\nDate: Fri Dec 29 20:34:43 2023\n\nWe apply the shifted composition rule -- an information-theoretic principle introduced in our earlier work [AC23] -- to establish shift Harnack inequalities for the Langevin diffusion. We obtain sharp constants for these inequalities for the first time, allowing us to investigate their relationship with other properties of the diffusion. Namely, we show that they are equivalent to a sharp \"local gradient-entropy\" bound, and that they imply curvature upper bounds in a compelling reflection of the Bakry-Emery theory of curvature lower bounds. Finally, we show that the local gradient-entropy inequality implies optimal concentration of the score, a.k.a. the logarithmic gradient of the density.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient (https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/spapps/v129y2019i12p5278-5311.html)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: last week;   * Dalalyan, Arnak S. ... In such a situation, we consider both deterministic and stochastic approximations of the gradient and provide an upper bound on the sampling error of the first-order LMC that quantifies the impact of the gradient evaluation inaccuracies. ... & Karagulyan, Avetik, 2019.\"User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient,\" Stochastic Processes and their Applications, Elsevier, vol. 129(12), pages 5278-5311.\n\n# User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient\n\n## Author\n\nListed:\n\n  * Dalalyan, Arnak S.\n  * Karagulyan, Avetik\n\nRegistered:\n\n  * Arnak Dalalyan\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we study the problem of sampling from a given probability density function that is known to be smooth and strongly log-concave. We analyze several methods of approximate sampling based on discretizations of the (highly overdamped) Langevin diffusion and establish guarantees on its error measured in the Wasserstein-2 distance. Our guarantees improve or extend the state-of-the-art results in three directions. First, we provide an upper bound on the error of the first-order Langevin Monte Carlo (LMC) algorithm with optimized varying step-size. This result has the advantage of being horizon free (we do not need to know in advance the target precision) and to improve by a logarithmic factor the corresponding result for the constant step-size. Second, we study the case where accurate evaluations of the gradient of the log-density are unavailable, but one can have access to approximations of the aforementioned gradient. In such a situation, we consider both deterministic and stochastic approximations of the gradient and provide an upper bound on the sampling error of the first-order LMC that quantifies the impact of the gradient evaluation inaccuracies. Third, we establish upper bounds for two versions of the second-order LMC, which leverage the Hessian of the log-density. We provide non asymptotic guarantees on the sampling error of these second-order LMCs. These guarantees reveal that the second-order LMC algorithms improve on the first-order LMC in ill-conditioned settings.\n\n## Suggested Citation\n\n* Dalalyan, Arnak S. & Karagulyan, Avetik, 2019. \"User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient,\" Stochastic Processes and their Applications, Elsevier, vol. 129(12), pages 5278-5311.\nHandle: RePEc:eee:spapps:v:129:y:2019:i:12:p:5278-5311\nDOI: 10.1016/j.spa.2019.02.016\n\n## Download full text from publisher\n\n[Input][Input]File URL: http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304414918304824\nDownload Restriction: Full text for ScienceDirect subscribers only\n\nAs the access to this document is restricted, you may want to look for a different version below or\n\n[Input][Button: search]\n\nfor a different version of it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient (https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.00095)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.0 years ago; Title: User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient ... Dalalyan, Avetik G.Karagulyan ... In such a situation, we consider both deterministic and stochastic approximations of the gradient and provide an upper bound on the sampling error of the first-order LMC that quantifies the impact of the gradient evaluation inaccuracies.\nTitle: User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient\nAuthors: Arnak S. Dalalyan, Avetik G. Karagulyan\nDate: Fri Sep 29 21:15:03 2017\n\nIn this paper, we study the problem of sampling from a given probability density function that is known to be smooth and strongly log-concave. We analyze several methods of approximate sampling based on discretizations of the (highly overdamped) Langevin diffusion and establish guarantees on its error measured in the Wasserstein-2 distance. Our guarantees improve or extend the state-of-the-art results in three directions. First, we provide an upper bound on the error of the first-order Langevin Monte Carlo (LMC) algorithm with optimized varying step-size. This result has the advantage of being horizon free (we do not need to know in advance the target precision) and to improve by a logarithmic factor the corresponding result for the constant step-size. Second, we study the case where accurate evaluations of the gradient of the log-density are unavailable, but one can have access to approximations of the aforementioned gradient. In such a situation, we consider both deterministic and stochastic approximations of the gradient and provide an upper bound on the sampling error of the first-order LMC that quantifies the impact of the gradient evaluation inaccuracies. Third, we establish upper bounds for two versions of the second-order LMC, which leverage the Hessian of the log-density. We provide nonasymptotic guarantees on the sampling error of these second-order LMCs. These guarantees reveal that the second-order LMC algorithms improve on the first-order LMC in ill-conditioned settings.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShifted Composition II: Shift Harnack Inequalities (https://payshopp.net/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2401.00071%23oiIsiUa7YGXxkxUiKzZPY60%3D)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Shifted Composition II: Shift Harnack Inequalities ... Altschuler ... Sinho Chewi ... that the local gradient-entropy inequality implies optimal concentration of the score, a.k.a. the\nShifted Composition II: Shift Harnack Inequalities\nand Curvature Upper Bounds\nJason M. Altschuler\nUPenn\nalts@upenn.edu\nSinho Chewi\nIAS\nschewi@ias.edu\nAbstract\nWe apply the shifted composition rule—an information-theoretic principle introduced in our\nearlier work [AC23]—to establish shift Harnack inequalities for the Langevin diﬀusion.\nWe\nobtain sharp constants for these inequalities for the ﬁrst time, allowing us to investigate their\nrelationship with other properties of the diﬀusion. Namely, we show that they are equivalent\nto a sharp “local gradient-entropy” bound, and that they imply curvature upper bounds in a\ncompelling reﬂection of the Bakry–´Emery theory of curvature lower bounds. Finally, we show\nthat the local gradient-entropy inequality implies optimal concentration of the score, a.k.a. the\nlogarithmic gradient of the density.\nContents\n1\nIntroduction\n2\n2\nPreliminaries\n5\n2.1\nInformation theory . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/320179990_User-friendly_guarantees_for_the_Langevin_Monte_Carlo_with_inaccurate_gradient)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient ... DOI:10.1016/j.spa.2019.02.016 ... More importantly, we establish non-asymptotic guarantees for the accuracy of a version of the Langevin Monte Carlo algorithm that is based on inaccurate evaluations of the gradient. ... Dalalyan, Avetik Karagulyan ... area of research in optimization and machine learning. ... deterministic approximations of integrals or diﬀerentials. ... If the mean squared error of the stochastic gradient is uniformly bounded in Ω, we can use [38,Theorem 4] to obtain an error bound with respect to the desired posterior π.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# User-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient\n\n  * September 2017\n  * Stochastic Processes and their Applications 129(12)\n\nDOI:10.1016/j.spa.2019.02.016\n\nAuthors:\n\nArnak Dalalyan\n\n  * ENSAE Paris\n\nAvetik Karagulyan\n\n  * King Abdullah University of Science and Technology\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nIn this paper, we revisit the recently established theoretical guarantees for the convergence of the Langevin Monte Carlo algorithm of sampling from a smooth and (strongly) log-concave density. We improve, in terms of constants, the existing results when the accuracy of sampling is measured in the Wasserstein distance and provide further insights on relations between, on the one hand, the Langevin Monte Carlo for sampling and, on the other hand, the gradient descent for optimization. More importantly, we establish non-asymptotic guarantees for the accuracy of a version of the Langevin Monte Carlo algorithm that is based on inaccurate evaluations of the gradient. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nShifted Composition I: Harnack and Reverse Transport Inequalities (https://payshopp.net/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2311.14520%23oiIsiUa7YGXxkxUiKzZPY60%3D)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Shifted Composition I: Harnack and Reverse Transport Inequalities ... Altschuler ... Sinho Chewi ... from geometric analysis with the discrete-time shifted divergence technique from differential\nShifted Composition I: Harnack and Reverse Transport Inequalities\nJason M. Altschuler\nUPenn\nalts@upenn.edu\nSinho Chewi\nIAS\nschewi@ias.edu\nAbstract\nWe formulate a new information-theoretic principle—the shifted composition rule—which\nbounds the divergence (e.g., Kullback–Leibler or R´enyi) between the laws of two stochastic\nprocesses via the introduction of auxiliary shifts. In this paper, we apply this principle to prove\nreverse transport inequalities for diffusions which, by duality, imply F.-Y. Wang’s celebrated\ndimension-free Harnack inequalities. Our approach bridges continuous-time coupling methods\nfrom geometric analysis with the discrete-time shifted divergence technique from differential\nprivacy and sampling. It also naturally gives rise to (1) an alternative continuous-time coupling\nmethod based on optimal transport, which bypasses Girsanov transformations, (2) functional\ninequalities for discrete-time processes, and (3) a family of “reverse” Harnack inequalities.\nContents\n1\nIntroduction\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser-friendly guarantees for the Langevin Monte Carlo with inaccurate gradient (https://explore.metascienceobservatory.org/papers/W2963599479)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 28 2015 • 14,090 citations Real and Complex Analysis.1987 • 11,498 citations Introductory